                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2fde22e3-d773-4e27-8dd6-fa3d097a8042",
                        "source_entry_id": "69c11083-c241-34be-8bf8-216f665740b6",
                        "source_sense_id": "83ba9938-c46d-38ad-9a93-2b9a5b4159f7",
                        "target_lang_word": "titrate",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "titrate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "15506e22-7d10-3c53-b426-69b8fec7c5d1",
                                        "entry_id": "f2a619e1-265c-3fe8-9db4-d19dd8419731",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To ascertain the amount of a constituent in a solution (or other mixture) by measuring the volume of a known concentration (the \\\"standard solution\\\") needed to complete a reaction."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c6d204b-4ae1-4236-a0ea-73e4b80c46fc",
                                                "sense_id": "15506e22-7d10-3c53-b426-69b8fec7c5d1",
                                                "text": "He was obsessed with making LSD even purer than Sandoz, producing it first in powder form in gelatin capsules as well as light-blue liquid (“Mother's Milk”) that was easily recognizable when titrated onto sugar cubes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cd8efd7d-5b87-3f00-a676-473ce9ce204d",
                                        "entry_id": "f2a619e1-265c-3fe8-9db4-d19dd8419731",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To adjust the amount of a drug consumed until the desired effects are achieved."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f79994bd-9895-439c-ba3c-4aec53a868d0",
                                                "sense_id": "cd8efd7d-5b87-3f00-a676-473ce9ce204d",
                                                "text": "A 5mg dose could not ease the pain, so he titrated to 10mg which brought him immediate relief."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b8c85d63-216e-3353-90d0-4b38a9274142",
                                        "entry_id": "f2a619e1-265c-3fe8-9db4-d19dd8419731",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To precisely control."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2b63f7ee-0b7f-4a8f-a86d-6b65196af5e0",
                                                "sense_id": "b8c85d63-216e-3353-90d0-4b38a9274142",
                                                "text": "In crisis debriefing, because it is done in group sessions, you can’t titrate the amount of exposure to the individual, so some may get more upset."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d3be54b0-513c-4ba1-b241-32e017eda910",
                                                "sense_id": "b8c85d63-216e-3353-90d0-4b38a9274142",
                                                "text": "\"All mammals engage in dangerous play,\" Gray told me. \"Dangerous play is how kids learn how to titrate fear.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "337e27f7-b7ce-4c8d-a04e-252b83835126",
                                                "sense_id": "b8c85d63-216e-3353-90d0-4b38a9274142",
                                                "text": "The error lies in thinking that one can titrate the application of violence to achieve exquisitely precise results. To the extent that the West continues to attempt to do so, it will merely ensure more mass graves like those of Bucha and Izyum, and more soldiers lying limbless or in the burn wards of Ukrainian military hospitals."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "titrate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "18842ce0-2131-3a22-8e61-37c863a11727",
                                        "entry_id": "c1c23a9d-ae2e-36b8-96eb-968610516ce0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A solution of unknown concentration which is (to be) tested by means of titration."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0f27e16e-e7a7-4d08-bca3-0ba0238fd589",
                                                "sense_id": "18842ce0-2131-3a22-8e61-37c863a11727",
                                                "text": "A typical titration requires approximately 1.8–2.0 mL of titrate at a concentration of 1–2 µM. The titrant will typically be at a concentration 10–20 times that of the titrate."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "titrate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d5a3c3fa-5fa7-31e3-9b64-0b78a2812a2d",
                                        "entry_id": "8874fd42-6f93-3536-8ec9-02bc6c97e30b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Precisely measured."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75ccf36e-0816-4e6c-9fdf-f09a510fe7d0",
                                                "sense_id": "d5a3c3fa-5fa7-31e3-9b64-0b78a2812a2d",
                                                "text": "Dr. R. Dubois's apparatus for applying anæsthetics composed of titrate mixtures of chloroform and air was described on June 22nd..before the Academy of Sciences."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
